g other ones though  a one-off windfall tax on oil companies  and I understand there was some dilemma  within the shadow cabinet within jeremy  corbyn's cabinets about that fearing  that it might damage the north sea oil  industry but nevertheless it is in there  although not in very specific terms  how does he propose to pay for all of  this poor well he insists that it's  fully costed in addition to this  document  there was another document to kind of  great pamphlets the estimate of a cannot  economists is that it's going to cost  the equivalent of around ninety billion  dollars to pay for this plan but the  Labour Party says that they have gone  through it with a fine-tooth comb that  they have the funds that they have the  tax proposals to actually fund this  fully and they are saying that this is  not some kind of magic money tree but  they actually do have the proposals to  do it now what was interesting though is  in proportionate in proportion to the  radical nature of this document what  they're saying is that judge it by the  opposition that it generates it's quite  a radical strategy to sell it to the  public effectively they're saying all of  those critics and however loudly they  shout about this and try to say that  it's rubbish and that it won't work that  will be evidence of just how good it is  so it's an interesting an interesting  tactic that the Labour Party are putting  forward for this to win over voters  ahead of that vote on December 12th or  many thanks indeed Paul Brennan there  live in Birmingham Chris Stafford is a  researcher at the Center for British  politics at the University of Nottingham  he joins us now via Skype from  Nottingham good to have you with us  Chris Labour running well behind the  Conservatives at the moment in the  opinion polls will this manifesto change  their fortunes well it remains to be  seen really doesn't it I think there  certainly certainly pulling out all the  stops to turn it around  yeah well it's defini